# The distribution header reference from plan/00-foundation/01-vision-and-scope.md.
#
# Three circuits: a heat source on its own loop, and two consumers tapped off a supply/return header
# pair. Every consumer runs cooler than the header, so each blends header water with its own return.
#
# `F-21`: the source has a loop of its own rather than sitting in the header path. Without it the
# source's flow *is* the network's flow, the plant has no freedom left to regulate with, and the
# system is rank deficient -- measured, 37 unknowns and rank 36.
#
# `F-22`: that loop is an **uncontrolled bypass**, not a three-way valve. A diverting valve
# recirculating into a fixed-duty source cannot change the source's outlet temperature at all --
# T_out = T_return + Q/(m*cp) for every recirculation rate -- so a valve here would regulate nothing
# and leave its own position constrained by nothing thermal. Holding a supply setpoint needs a mixing
# valve and a controller, which is a later tier.
#
# `HS1` states no `out`, and must not: with the duty and the outlet both stated the source flow is
# pinned to the network draw, the bypass carries exactly zero, and a zero-flow branch is `S-21`'s
# singularity. The outlet floats and the primary flow is `PU_MAIN`'s own.
#
# `S-39`: the pressure datum is on `N2`, which lies inside a branch, not on a junction. A pressure
# stated on a node that carries a mass balance is read as a boundary admitting unknown mass flux, so
# writing it on `N1` would quietly turn this closed circuit into an open one, drop no redundant mass
# balance, and leave the system deficient by exactly one.
#
# `F-16`/`F-17`: each consumer's recirculation leg is connected, so `in=50` on a 60 C header is a
# demand its valve can actually meet. Both ends of each attachment are written out because
# `supply`/`return` cannot express a mixing subcircuit: the rule takes the first unconnected inlet,
# and on this shape that is the valve's outlet, which wires the consumer's return to the *supply*
# header and leaves every header branch carrying zero.
#
# `C-66`: the tap branches carry real pipework. A branch written as a bare node-to-node connection
# drops 0 kPa, and a valve sized against no resistance at all sits inside the solver's smoothing band,
# so the rule declines rather than inventing an answer.

fluidscript 1
project static plant_01

circuit heating 100
fluid water

HS1     heat_exchanger power=54                   # the plant-side source; its outlet floats
PU_MAIN pump head=10                              # constant speed, so the primary flow is its own
BV      valve kv=2                                # the bypass takes the primary's surplus
PB      pipe length=4 dn=32

connections
N1 - HS1 - N2 - PU_MAIN - N3
N3 - PB - BV - N1                                 # what the network does not draw, recirculated
N3 - N4                                           # supply header
N6 - N5
N5 - N1                                           # return header

N2 node p=250

circuit AHU 101

HE_AHU  heat_exchanger in=50 out=30 power=24 kW
TV_AHU  three_way_valve
PU_AHU  pump
PA1     pipe length=12 dn=25
PA2     pipe length=12 dn=25

connections
NM_AHU - PU_AHU - HE_AHU - TV_AHU
TV_AHU.b - NM_AHU                                 # recirculation, 30 C back to the mixing node
N3 - PA1 - NM_AHU                                 # drawn from the supply header
TV_AHU.a - PA2 - N5                               # and returned to it

circuit radiators 102

HE_RAD  heat_exchanger in=50 out=30 power=30 kW
TV_RAD  three_way_valve
PU_RAD  pump
PR1     pipe length=18 dn=25
PR2     pipe length=18 dn=25

connections
NM_RAD - PU_RAD - HE_RAD - TV_RAD
TV_RAD.b - NM_RAD
N4 - PR1 - NM_RAD
TV_RAD.a - PR2 - N6
